---
/**
 * The method, in first person.
 *
 * Rewritten August 2026 against a voice audit. The old version was accurate
 * and sounded like nobody wrote it: royal "we" throughout, an em dash every
 * other sentence, and a neat epigram closing each section. For a page whose
 * whole argument is "a person went and checked", having no person in it was a
 * structural contradiction.
 *
 * Constraints this page is written to, and the next ones should be too:
 *   - first person singular for anything I actually did
 *   - em dashes at roughly one per 300 words, not one per two sentences
 *   - no three-item lists in consecutive paragraphs
 *   - no aphorism resolving a section; end on the fact and stop
 *   - paragraph lengths vary hard, one-sentence paragraphs allowed
 *   - the mistakes are named, with dates, because they are the evidence
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Who writes this site?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "Matt Werth, a software engineer and photographer who travels the US to see parks. I make the calls, read the source documents, and publish corrections under my name with the date.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Where do the facts come from?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "From the body that sets them: Maryland DNR, the county, the town. Never from another guide or a review site. A community write-up can point me at something but cannot carry a page on its own.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What happens when something is wrong?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "I fix it, date it, and leave the mistake on the page saying what it used to say. Corrections are published, not deleted.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How I check this — Deep Creek Field Guide"
  description="Who writes this site, where the facts come from, and the three things I have already got wrong and corrected in public. Every fact carries a date."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">Method</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[13ch] text-slate">How I check this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[56ch]">
      I go to whoever sets the rule, write down the date I asked, and publish it
      when I get it wrong.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      I'm Matt Werth — an engineer and a photographer who drives out to Garrett
      County from Delaware, usually with kids in the car. I don't live there,
      which is exactly why this site runs on phone calls rather than on what I
      remember about a park I saw two summers ago.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Swallow Falls started requiring a day-use reservation in May. Months on,
      plenty of pages still tell you to turn up and park. From Baltimore that
      mistake costs you three hours.
    </p>

    <h2>The checklist</h2>

    <ol class="list-none p-0 m-0 my-6 sheet breakout divide-y-2 divide-lake/25">
      <li class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3.5">
        <p class="label m-0">The source is whoever sets the rule</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[58ch]">
          The agency that runs the park, the town that sets the hours, whoever
          takes the money. Never another guide, even when it's probably right.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3.5">
        <p class="label m-0">Every fact says how solid it is</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[58ch]">
          Confirmed, or read from the source. The two are labeled separately
          rather than blurred into one word — see below.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3.5">
        <p class="label m-0">Guides and review sites never publish here</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[58ch]">
          A fact whose only origin is a write-up stays out of search until
          someone checks it. The build enforces that, not my memory.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3.5">
        <p class="label m-0">"Not confirmed" is a real answer</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[58ch]">
          A blank means I haven't checked it. It does not mean the park lacks
          the thing, and rendering it as "No" would invent facts ten at a time.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3.5">
        <p class="label m-0">When sources disagree, whoever sets the rule wins</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[58ch]">
          No averaging. Garrett County says Swallow Falls needs a reservation
          every day; Maryland DNR says weekends and holidays. DNR runs the park,
          so DNR wins and the county's version sits on the page as a recorded
          conflict.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-3.5" id="corrections">
        <p class="label m-0">I publish the mistakes</p>
        <p class="m-0 mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[58ch]">
          Fixed, dated, with the old claim left on the page saying what it used
          to say.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The two labels</h2>

    <dl class="sheet breakout my-6 divide-y-2 divide-lake/25">
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-flag-green-bearing">Confirmed</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          I called, visited, or asked the people who set the rule. The stamp
          names them and gives the date.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-contour-deep">Read from the source</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          Taken from a named primary source and linked, so you can check it
          yourself. Accurate as far as it goes, and not the same as having
          asked.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Most of this site is currently the second kind. I don't take free
      admission, tell a place I'm coming, or run paid listings, and I pay my own
      park fees.
    </p>

    <h2>Three I've already got wrong</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <strong>Swallow Falls reservations.</strong> I published "every day" on
      the county's announcement. DNR runs the park and says weekends and
      holidays. A reader caught it.
      <a href="/parks/swallow-falls/#corrected">The correction is on the page</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <strong>The 4-H center.</strong> I had it as a lake open daily for
      swimming and canoeing. The people who run it put both on the private side,
      for booked groups only. My version would have sent a family out there to
      swim in water they couldn't reach.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      <strong>Three parks marked "believed free".</strong> Believed by whom? I
      hadn't checked. That's a guess written in a soft enough voice to look like
      a fact, which is the exact thing this site exists to refuse.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The 4-H error is the one worth naming a cause for. Its only source was a
      tourism website, and the build gate at the time checked that <em>some</em>
      source existed rather than a primary one — so rule three was decoration
      for about a week. The gate requires a primary source now, and three thin
      records dropped out of search the moment I fixed it.
    </p>

    <h2>Found something wrong?</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Tell me. A note from someone who stood there yesterday beats another phone
      call, and there's a report box at the bottom of every park page. Or reach
      me through <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth Design</a>,
      my studio, which builds and pays for this.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/verified/">Every fact on the site, with its source and age</a></li>
      <li><a href="/parks/">The state parks</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
